// THE LEG THAT STARTS A JOB, WHICH IS THE HALF OF PRINTING THAT WAS NEVER IN THE SUBSTRATE.
//
// gunbc.fleet.printer_project_delivery puts a sliced project on the machine over FTPS and stops
// there, because a Bambu printer in LAN-only mode does not begin a job when a file lands on its
// card. Something has to publish a start command over MQTT, and until now that something was a
// hand-written client run beside the modeled path -- the out-of-band actuation DESIGN section 6
// names, and the reason the operator's standing request was that the whole chain run through .dag.
//
// THE ORDER IS A REAL DEPENDENCY AND NOT A CONVENTION. The start command names a file by SD-card
// path; it does not carry the payload. Publishing it before delivery completes asks the printer to
// print a file that is not there, and the printer's refusal arrives on the report topic rather than
// as a failed publish -- so the publish would succeed and the job would not exist. That is why this
// function takes the remote name from a delivery RECEIPT rather than as a caller-supplied string:
// the only way to name a file here is to have moved it.
//
// THE CREDENTIAL PATH IS THE SAME ONE DELIVERY WALKS, AND DELIBERATELY THE SAME CODE.
// printer -> locus -> Secret Manager -> owner-only binding -> publish -> shred. It is not a second
// resolution written to look like the first: printer_endpoint_address and
// observe_printer_secret_container are imported from the delivery module rather than re-derived,
// because two functions answering "where is this printer and which secret holds its code" is the
// meaning fork that lets one be hardened while the other stays wrong. What differs is only what the
// credential is spent on, and that difference is one argv builder.
//
// gunbc.fleet_printer_access FORBIDS FALLING BACK TO THE OPERATOR'S FILES ON srv2 WHEN RESOLUTION
// REFUSES, and this module has no arm that does. Every refusal below ends the attempt.

type PrinterSerialLookup
  = PrinterSerialFound { serial: NonEmptyStr }
  | PrinterSerialUnknown { printer: PhysicalAssetIdentity }
  | PrinterSerialUnrecorded { printer: PhysicalAssetIdentity }
  | PrinterSerialAmbiguous { printer: PhysicalAssetIdentity }

// THE TOPIC IS KEYED ON THE SERIAL, SO THE SERIAL IS RESOLVED FROM THE INVENTORY RATHER THAN PASSED
// IN. A caller-supplied serial beside a caller-supplied printer identity is the same two-facts-free-
// to-disagree hole the delivery module already found and closed on its endpoint: it would let
// `printer_02 + printer_01's serial` fetch one machine's access code and address the other. The
// roster is joined on the identity, and a printer with no recorded serial refuses rather than
// falling back to a spelling derived by convention.
//
// owned_printer_inventory LIVES IN A PRODUCT MODULE AND IS CONSUMED FROM HERE RATHER THAN COPIED.
// Its home is arguable -- it reads as a fleet fact -- but the only structural law on the import
// graph is acyclicity, and a second roster of owned printers would be the duplication that actually
// costs something. Relocating it is a separate change with its own census.
fn printer_serial(printer: PhysicalAssetIdentity) -> PrinterSerialLookup {
  let matches = owned_printer_inventory |> filter(a => a.identity == printer)
  match matches |> first {
    Absent => PrinterSerialUnknown { printer: printer }
    Present { value: asset } =>
      if (matches |> length) != 1 {
        PrinterSerialAmbiguous { printer: printer }
      } else {
        match asset.physical_serial {
          Absent => PrinterSerialUnrecorded { printer: printer }
          Present { value: s } => PrinterSerialFound { serial: s }
        }
      }
  }
}

// THE PINNED CA, FETCHED AND VERIFIED BY DIGEST BEFORE IT IS TRUSTED.
//
// The transport must verify the printer's certificate, and the CA the PRINTER SERVES cannot be the
// thing that verifies it -- that accepts any peer presenting a consistent chain of its own making.
// So the bundle comes from an independent distributor and its bytes are checked against a digest
// this repository recorded, which is what makes the pin evidence rather than a download.
//
// THE SEAL IS WHAT THE ARGV BUILDER REQUIRES. If mosquitto_publish_command took a bare FilePath, a
// caller could hand it any file -- including one written from the connection it is about to verify
// -- and the emitted argv would be identical to the safe one. That is exactly the gap
// gunbc.auth.netrc_binding records for credentials, in its other direction: there the sealed thing
// is a secret, here it is a trust anchor, and in both cases an unsealed path makes the guarantee
// unobservable at the call site.
type PinnedCaBundle sole_constructor {
  path: FilePath
}

// THE ATTEMPT IDENTITY IS THE WORKSPACE'S NAME, WHICH IS WHERE THE UNIQUENESS ACTUALLY LIVES.
//
// mktemp -d creates a directory the kernel guarantees did not already exist, so its name is distinct
// from that of every attempt whose workspace is SIMULTANEOUSLY ALLOCATED on this filesystem. That is
// the whole guarantee, and it is deliberately narrower than the one this paragraph first claimed.
//
// THE EXCLUSION ENDS WHEN THE DIRECTORY DOES, WHICH IS BEFORE ANY REPORT IS READ. This leg removes
// the workspace immediately after the publish, folded into `cleaned`, and returns. So the window in
// which the filesystem namespace is actually preventing a collision spans mint -> publish -> cleanup,
// and NOT mint -> publish -> report reconciliation. A first draft of this note asserted the longer
// window on the grounds that a report can only belong to an attempt whose observer has not finished
// -- true, and not something the construction underwrites once the name has been released. Random
// mktemp naming makes reuse very unlikely, and "very unlikely" is exactly the better-odds-as-identity
// claim rejected two paragraphs up for the millisecond clock. Rejecting it there and accepting it
// here would be the same error with a different entropy source.
//
// WHAT HOLDS TODAY is therefore: two attempts whose publishes overlap cannot share an identity. What
// does NOT hold is that an attempt which published, cleaned up, and whose report is still in flight
// cannot be aliased by a later attempt. Nothing consumes the report topic yet, so nothing currently
// depends on the stronger property -- which is why this is a stated boundary rather than a defect.
//
// NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, NAMING THE CAPABILITY AND NOT AN ARTIFACT: the identity's allocation must be
// owned by the whole publish-then-observe transaction rather than by the publish alone, so the
// workspace (or a dedicated name reservation) is released only once report attribution has
// terminated. A report-topic observer may not be built against the longer window until that lifetime
// join exists; building one first would make an unheld guarantee load-bearing, which is the shape
// this module has now corrected three times on this one field.
//
// The scope is narrow in two further ways worth stating rather than discovering: two attempts on
// different hosts, or separated by a cleanup of /tmp, may reuse a name.
//
// IT READS THE WORKSPACE THAT ALREADY EXISTS INSTEAD OF MINTING A SECOND ONE. A dedicated mktemp
// would be a second directory to create, remove, and account for on every arm -- and its removal
// result would be a third cleanup signal that either joins `cleaned` or gets dropped. The CA
// workspace is already created, already removed, and already folded into `cleaned`, so taking the
// identity from it adds no effect and no untracked residue. It also means a failed mint is already
// refused: CaBundleRefused fires when no workspace could be created, before anything is published.
fn start_attempt_identity(workspace: FilePath) -> NonEmptyStr {
  workspace as NonEmptyStr
}

fn pinned_ca_bundle_path(bundle: PinnedCaBundle) -> FilePath { bundle.path }

type CaBundleAcquisition
  = CaBundlePinned { bundle: PinnedCaBundle, workspace: FilePath }
  | CaBundleRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }
  | CaBundleRefusedResidueRemains { cause: NonEmptyStr }

// A DIGEST MISMATCH REFUSES AND DOES NOT RETRY ANOTHER SOURCE. There is no second source to fall
// back to: the alternatives are the printer's own chain, which is circular, and skipping
// verification, which is the absorbing fallback DESIGN section 5 forbids -- a failure arm that
// widens instead of stopping. The bytes either match what was recorded or the leg ends.
fn acquire_pinned_ca_bundle() -> CaBundleAcquisition
  uses net: Network
{
  let created = shell.Mktemp.Dir()
  if created.success == false {
    CaBundleRefused { cause: "no workspace could be created for the pinned CA bundle" }
  } else {
    let destination = concat(created.path as String, "/printer_ca.pem")
    match run_shell_command_capture(
      command: curl_download_command(
        url: uri_wire(uri: bambu_printer_ca_bundle_source.uri) as String,
        destination: destination),
      transport: LocalExec
    ) {
      ProcessRefused { exit_code: _, stderr: stderr } =>
        ca_bundle_fetch_refusal(
          workspace: created.path as FilePath,
          what_failed: concat("the pinned CA bundle could not be downloaded: ", stderr))
      ProcessOutputAbsent =>
        verify_pinned_ca_bundle(workspace: created.path as FilePath, destination: destination)
      ProcessOutputPresent { text: _ } =>
        verify_pinned_ca_bundle(workspace: created.path as FilePath, destination: destination)
    }
  }
}

fn verify_pinned_ca_bundle(workspace: FilePath, destination: String) -> CaBundleAcquisition
{
  match sha256sum_file_digest_via_shell(path: destination as NonEmptyStr) {
    Sha256FileDigestUnavailable { path: _, reason: reason } =>
      ca_bundle_fetch_refusal(
        workspace: workspace,
        what_failed: concat("the downloaded CA bundle could not be digested: ", reason))
    Sha256FileDigest { digest: digest } =>
      if (digest.hex as String) == (bambu_printer_ca_bundle_sha256 as String) {
        CaBundlePinned {
          bundle: PinnedCaBundle { path: destination as FilePath },
          workspace: workspace,
        }
      } else {
        ca_bundle_fetch_refusal(
          workspace: workspace,
          what_failed: "the downloaded CA bundle does not match the recorded digest, so it is not the anchor this repository verified against the certificate the printer serves")
      }
  }
}

fn ca_bundle_fetch_refusal(workspace: FilePath, what_failed: String) -> CaBundleAcquisition {
  if shell.Remove.RecursiveForce(path: workspace).success {
    CaBundleRefused { cause: what_failed as NonEmptyStr }
  } else {
    CaBundleRefusedResidueRemains {
      cause: concat(what_failed, "; the workspace could not be removed either") as NonEmptyStr,
    }
  }
}

// THE START RECEIPT NAMES WHAT WAS PUBLISHED AND WHERE, and is minted only inside the arm of a real
// publish that exited zero. A publish is not a print: mosquitto_pub returning zero establishes that
// the broker accepted the packet, and the printer reports whether it accepted the JOB on a separate
// topic this leg does not read. The receipt is named for what it can support.
type PrintStartReceipt sole_constructor {
  printer: PhysicalAssetIdentity
  topic: MqttTopic
  remote_name: NonEmptyStr
  sequence_id: NonEmptyStr
}

// A LOST CONNECTION IS NOT A REFUSAL, AND THIS ARM EXISTS BECAUSE THE FIRST WET RUN GOT IT WRONG IN
// THE DIRECTION THAT MATTERS.
//
// The publish came back exit 7, "The connection was lost", after two minutes, and this module
// reported PrintStartRefused -- "the start command was not published". The printer was in fact
// printing: gcode_state RUNNING on the exact digest-named .3mf the run had just uploaded. At QoS 1 a
// client waits for a PUBACK it may never see, and the broker may have taken ownership of the message
// long before the socket died, so a transport loss says nothing whatever about whether the action
// occurred. Reporting it as a refusal is the same commit-ambiguity collapse the mutation path
// already refuses to make for a 5xx, in the more dangerous direction: it asserts that nothing
// happened, which is precisely the premise a retry is built on.
//
// AND THE RETRY IS THE HARM. This module has no automatic one, and start_may_be_replayed exists to
// keep it that way: a second project_file on a machine already printing is the duplicate actuation
// that AtLeastOnce admits and that nothing in the modeled Bambu protocol is known to suppress. So
// the unknown arm is terminal, exactly as the deletion's is, and it is reconciled by READING the
// report topic rather than by publishing again.
//
// THE EXIT CODE IS NOT CONSULTED, DELIBERATELY. mosquitto_pub does distinguish an auth failure from
// a lost connection, and a table mapping its codes to "definitely did not publish" would let this
// arm shrink -- but that table is one I have not measured, and inferring it from the shape of the
// situation is what produced the wrong certificate claim two files over. Every non-zero exit is
// unknown until something establishes otherwise.
type PrintStart
  = PrintStartPublished { receipt: PrintStartReceipt }
  | PrintStartOutcomeUnknown { printer: PhysicalAssetIdentity, cause: NonEmptyStr }
  | PrintStartOutcomeUnknownResidueRemains { printer: PhysicalAssetIdentity, cause: NonEmptyStr }
  | PrintStartRefused { printer: PhysicalAssetIdentity, cause: NonEmptyStr }
  | PrintStartPublishedCleanupRefused { receipt: PrintStartReceipt, cause: NonEmptyStr }
  | PrintStartRefusedResidueRemains { printer: PhysicalAssetIdentity, cause: NonEmptyStr }

// NO ARM AUTHORIZES A SECOND PUBLISH, and this is written as a total function over the coproduct so
// that adding an arm without deciding its replay semantics does not compile.
fn start_may_be_replayed(o: PrintStart) -> Bool {
  match o {
    PrintStartPublished { receipt: _ } => false
    PrintStartOutcomeUnknown { printer: _, cause: _ } => false
    PrintStartOutcomeUnknownResidueRemains { printer: _, cause: _ } => false
    PrintStartRefused { printer: _, cause: _ } => false
    PrintStartPublishedCleanupRefused { receipt: _, cause: _ } => false
    PrintStartRefusedResidueRemains { printer: _, cause: _ } => false
  }
}

// THE SEQUENCE ID IDENTIFIES THE ATTEMPT, NOT THE PAYLOAD, AND IT IS NO LONGER A PARAMETER.
//
// The orchestrator passed the delivery receipt's remote_name for both, and remote_name is derived
// from the payload's DIGEST -- so every legitimate reprint of the same .3mf carries the same
// sequence_id. Bambu echoes that value on the report topic, and it is the only handle an observer
// has for joining a report to the command that caused it, so a payload-keyed id lets a report from
// an earlier print of the same gauge be attributed to a later invocation. Two things that must be
// free to differ were spelled from one source.
//
// A caller-supplied id would only move the defect: the caller is where the conflation happened. It
// is minted here, from the clock, because "which attempt" is a fact about THIS execution and there
// is nothing at the call site that knows it better.
//
// A TIMESTAMP IS NOT AN ATTEMPT IDENTITY, AND THE FIRST REPAIR HERE ONLY LOOKED LIKE ONE.
//
// The identity itself is start_attempt_identity, declared beside the CA workspace it reads. The
// previous revision minted the id from clock_now_probed_at, which forwards Clock.Now().timestamp,
// which is emitted as `date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ` -- ONE-SECOND granularity. Two legitimate start
// attempts in the same second alias, so the value carried the identity property in its name and not
// in its construction. That is the same shape as the defect it was introduced to fix: the payload
// digest was a stable name for the wrong subject, and a second-resolution clock is a distinct name
// for the wrong grain. Reaching for Clock.UnixMillis would only shrink the window; a finer clock
// reduces the collision domain and never structurally guarantees uniqueness, so it would leave the
// same overclaim with better odds.
//
// SO THE UNIQUENESS IS TAKEN FROM SOMETHING THAT ACTUALLY HAS IT. mktemp -d creates a directory the
// kernel guarantees did not already exist, so its name is distinct from every OTHER LIVE ATTEMPT's
// on this filesystem -- and that is exactly the population a report join can confuse, because a
// report can only be attributed to an attempt whose publish has happened and whose observer has not
// yet finished. The guarantee is scoped rather than absolute: two attempts on different hosts, or
// separated by a cleanup of /tmp, may reuse a name. That scope is stated because a report observer
// must be built against the guarantee that exists and not the one the field name suggests.
//
// THE DIRECTORY IS THE MINT AND IS REMOVED IMMEDIATELY. Nothing is stored in it; what is wanted is
// the kernel's exclusion, not the storage.
//
// A FAILED MINT ALREADY REFUSES, AND IT REFUSES BEFORE ANYTHING IS PUBLISHED. The workspace is the
// CA bundle's, so a directory that could not be created is CaBundleRefused, reached while the leg
// still has nothing on the wire. There is no arm that falls back to a constant spelling: one would
// collide across every attempt and hand an observer an id that looks like data. Refusing costs a
// print; publishing costs the ability to attribute the report -- and reconciliation by reading that
// report is the ONLY defence this leg has against the duplicate-actuation risk the protocol module
// states is unresolved. A start nobody can attribute is the one that must not be sent.
fn start_print_on_printer(
  printer: PhysicalAssetIdentity,
  remote_name: NonEmptyStr,
  token_source: AccessTokenSource,
  materializations: List<VerifiedVersionForAttempt>,
) -> PrintStart
  uses net: Network
{
  match printer_serial(printer: printer) {
    PrinterSerialUnknown { printer: p } =>
      PrintStartRefused { printer: p,
        cause: "this printer is not in the owned inventory, so the request topic its serial keys cannot be derived" }
    PrinterSerialUnrecorded { printer: p } =>
      PrintStartRefused { printer: p,
        cause: "this printer has no recorded serial, and the MQTT request topic is keyed on the serial: the start refuses rather than addressing a topic spelled by convention" }
    PrinterSerialAmbiguous { printer: p } =>
      PrintStartRefused { printer: p,
        cause: "more than one inventory row names this printer, so its serial is ambiguous and the start refuses rather than taking one by list order" }
    PrinterSerialFound { serial: serial } =>
  match printer_endpoint_address(printer: printer) {
    PrinterAddressUnknown { printer: p } =>
      PrintStartRefused { printer: p,
        cause: "no LAN endpoint is recorded for this printer in gunbc.fleet_intent_network" }
    PrinterAddressAmbiguous { printer: p } =>
      PrintStartRefused { printer: p,
        cause: "more than one LAN fabrication endpoint matches this printer; the roster is ambiguous and the start refuses rather than picking one by list order" }
    PrinterAddressWrongClass { printer: p } =>
      PrintStartRefused { printer: p,
        cause: "an endpoint names this printer but is not a LAN fabrication appliance, so it is not the control channel and the start refuses rather than authenticating to it" }
    PrinterAddressFound { address: lan_address } =>
  match observe_printer_secret_container(
    printer: printer, token_source: token_source, materializations: materializations
  ) {
    PrinterCredentialObservationRefused { printer: p, cause: cause } =>
      PrintStartRefused { printer: p, cause: cause }
    PrinterCredentialLocusUndeclared { printer: p } =>
      PrintStartRefused { printer: p,
        cause: "this printer has no access-code locus declared in gunbc.fleet_printer_access, so there is no secret to observe and the start refuses rather than reading the operator's file on srv2" }
    PrinterCredentialLocusConflicted { printer: p, matches: _ } =>
      PrintStartRefused { printer: p,
        cause: "more than one access-code locus is declared for this printer, so which secret holds its code is ambiguous and the start refuses" }
    PrinterCredentialMaterializationVanished { printer: p, exact: _ } =>
      PrintStartRefused { printer: p,
        cause: "a provisioning receipt records this printer's access code, but Secret Manager answers 404 for the container: the start refuses rather than treating a deletion as a printer that was never onboarded" }
    PrinterCredentialVersionUnrecorded { printer: p, container: _ } =>
      PrintStartRefused { printer: p,
        cause: "this printer's access code secret exists but no provisioning receipt records which version holds it, so the start refuses rather than guessing a version" }
    PrinterCredentialProvisionRequired { printer: p, desired: _ } =>
      PrintStartRefused { printer: p,
        cause: "this printer's access code has not been provisioned, so the start refuses: provisioning is its own operation and is not performed as a side effect of starting a job" }
    PrinterCredentialProvisionAmbiguous { printer: p, matches: _ } =>
      PrintStartRefused { printer: p,
        cause: "more than one provisioning receipt names this printer's secret, so the exact version is ambiguous and the start refuses" }
    PrinterCredentialProvisioned { exact: exact_ref } =>
      match fetch_secret_ref_credential(secret_ref: exact_ref, token_source: token_source) {
        SecretCredentialAccessUpsertRequired { plan: _ } =>
          PrintStartRefused { printer: printer,
            cause: "the access code could not be read: Secret Manager access has not been granted to this identity" }
        SecretCredentialWireDecodeRefused { identity_cause: _, payload_cause: _ } =>
          PrintStartRefused { printer: printer,
            cause: "the Secret Manager response for the access code did not decode" }
        SecretCredentialResolvedVersionMismatch { requested: requested, resolved: resolved } =>
          PrintStartRefused { printer: printer,
            cause: concat(concat("the access code fetch asked for ", requested),
              concat(" and the service answered about ", concat(resolved,
                "; the credential this start would authenticate with is not the one the receipt verified"))) as NonEmptyStr }
        SecretCredentialFetchRefused { reason: reason } =>
          PrintStartRefused { printer: printer, cause: reason }
        SecretCredentialReady { credential: credential, resolved_version: _ } =>
          publish_start_with_credential(
            printer: printer, serial: serial, lan_address: lan_address,
            remote_name: remote_name, credential: credential)
      }
  }
  }
  }
}

// THE CA IS ACQUIRED AFTER THE CREDENTIAL AND BEFORE THE BINDING, which is the ordering that leaves
// the least on disk. Fetching it first means every credential refusal above also has to clean up a
// downloaded bundle; placing the binding first means every CA refusal has to shred an access code.
// The two brackets are therefore nested rather than parallel, innermost being the secret.
fn publish_start_with_credential(
  printer: PhysicalAssetIdentity,
  serial: NonEmptyStr,
  lan_address: NonEmptyStr,
  remote_name: NonEmptyStr,
  credential: Secret,
) -> PrintStart
  uses net: Network
{
  match acquire_pinned_ca_bundle() {
    CaBundleRefused { cause: cause } => PrintStartRefused { printer: printer, cause: cause }
    CaBundleRefusedResidueRemains { cause: cause } =>
      PrintStartRefusedResidueRemains { printer: printer, cause: cause }
    CaBundlePinned { bundle: bundle, workspace: workspace } =>
      match place_mosquitto_options_privately(
        login: bambu_lan_mqtt_user, password: credential
      ) {
        MosquittoOptionsRefused { cause: cause } => {
          let dropped = shell.Remove.RecursiveForce(path: workspace).success
          if dropped {
            PrintStartRefused { printer: printer, cause: cause }
          } else {
            PrintStartRefusedResidueRemains { printer: printer,
              cause: concat(cause as String, "; the CA workspace could not be removed either") as NonEmptyStr }
          }
        }
        MosquittoOptionsRefusedResidueRemains { cause: cause } => {
          let dropped = shell.Remove.RecursiveForce(path: workspace).success
          PrintStartRefusedResidueRemains { printer: printer, cause: cause }
        }
        MosquittoOptionsPlaced { binding: binding } => {
          endpoint = bambu_lan_mqtt_endpoint(host: lan_address)
          topic = bambu_request_topic(serial: serial)
          command = bambu_start_command(remote_name: remote_name)
          outcome = run_shell_command_capture(
            command: env_prefixed_command(
              bindings: mosquitto_options_env(binding: binding),
              command: mosquitto_publish_command(
                endpoint: endpoint,
                topic: topic as NonEmptyStr,
                payload: bambu_start_payload_json(
                  command: command,
                  sequence_id: start_attempt_identity(workspace: workspace) as String),
                qos: bambu_start_command_qos,
                ca_bundle: pinned_ca_bundle_path(bundle: bundle))),
            transport: LocalExec
          )
          let credential_shredded = shred_mosquitto_options_binding(binding: binding)
          let workspace_dropped = shell.Remove.RecursiveForce(path: workspace).success
          let cleaned = credential_shredded && workspace_dropped
          classify_publish_outcome(
            printer: printer, outcome: outcome, topic: topic, remote_name: remote_name,
            sequence_id: start_attempt_identity(workspace: workspace), cleaned: cleaned)
        }
      }
  }
}

// THE CLEANUP RESULT IS NOT COLLAPSED INTO SUCCESS OR FAILURE, for the reason the delivery module
// already records: assigning the shred to `_` meant a completed publish whose access code stayed on
// disk returned the clean arm. A published start with residue is neither, and is spelled as neither.
fn start_receipt(
  printer: PhysicalAssetIdentity,
  topic: MqttTopic,
  remote_name: NonEmptyStr,
  sequence_id: NonEmptyStr,
  cleaned: Bool,
) -> PrintStart {
  let receipt = PrintStartReceipt {
    printer: printer, topic: topic, remote_name: remote_name, sequence_id: sequence_id,
  }
  if cleaned {
    PrintStartPublished { receipt: receipt }
  } else {
    PrintStartPublishedCleanupRefused {
      receipt: receipt,
      cause: "the start command was published but the credential shred or the CA workspace removal did not complete",
    }
  }
}

// THE WHOLE PROCESS OUTCOME IS CLASSIFIED HERE, AND PRODUCTION CLASSIFIES NOWHERE ELSE.
//
// The effect used to match ProcessOutcome inline, bind exit_code to `_`, and call start_refusal with
// the stderr alone. That left the enrolled witness pinning the HELPER's semantics rather than the
// fact it was written to protect: a later edit could branch on exit_code at the call site, produce
// PrintStartRefused for an auth failure, and every row in the witness file would stay green because
// none of them can see the call site. A witness is not a consumer, and a pure helper reached through
// an unwitnessed match is a helper whose caller is free to disagree with it.
//
// So the seam takes the ProcessOutcome ITSELF -- exit code included, deliberately unread -- and the
// effect's body is one call to it. The decision not to consult the exit code is now inside the
// thing the witness executes, which is what makes "every nonzero result is unknown" a claim about
// production rather than about a function production happens to use today.
fn classify_publish_outcome(
  printer: PhysicalAssetIdentity,
  outcome: ProcessOutcome,
  topic: MqttTopic,
  remote_name: NonEmptyStr,
  sequence_id: NonEmptyStr,
  cleaned: Bool,
) -> PrintStart {
  match outcome {
    ProcessRefused { exit_code: _, stderr: stderr } =>
      start_refusal(printer: printer, stderr: stderr, cleaned: cleaned)
    ProcessOutputAbsent =>
      start_receipt(printer: printer, topic: topic, remote_name: remote_name,
        sequence_id: sequence_id, cleaned: cleaned)
    ProcessOutputPresent { text: _ } =>
      start_receipt(printer: printer, topic: topic, remote_name: remote_name,
        sequence_id: sequence_id, cleaned: cleaned)
  }
}

// THE CAUSE NO LONGER CLAIMS THE COMMAND WAS NOT PUBLISHED, because that sentence was false on the
// one run that produced it. It says what is actually known -- the client did not confirm -- and it
// names the reconciliation, which is reading the report topic rather than publishing again.
fn start_refusal(printer: PhysicalAssetIdentity, stderr: String, cleaned: Bool) -> PrintStart {
  if cleaned {
    PrintStartOutcomeUnknown {
      printer: printer,
      cause: concat(concat(
        "the publish did not complete, so whether the printer received the start command is UNKNOWN and it may be printing: ",
        stderr),
        ". Reconcile by reading device/<serial>/report. Do NOT publish again: a second project_file on a machine already printing is a duplicate job.") as NonEmptyStr,
    }
  } else {
    PrintStartOutcomeUnknownResidueRemains {
      printer: printer,
      cause: concat(concat(
        "the publish did not complete AND the credential shred or CA workspace removal did not: ",
        stderr),
        ". Whether the printer received the start command is UNKNOWN and it may be printing; reconcile by reading the report topic, never by publishing again.") as NonEmptyStr,
    }
  }
}
